  "applies": true,
  "fixmes": [],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Applies.** `validate_rust_functions` (src/rust_coverage.rs:216-273) is a repository-validation function with a parameter named `errors: &mut Vec<String>` that collects validation errors. The report is built from them, so it is a site. It does not collect errors only to abort.\n\n**Obligations.** After a check fails, a site must still run every later check that does not depend on the failed check's result, including later checks in the same loop iteration. It must not report one failure twice.\n\n**Violation match, `continue` at line 247.**\n- It sits in the `Err` arm of `fs::read_to_string(&file)`, after pushing \"cannot read Rust source\".\n- The only later check in that iteration is `missing_function_references(&source, definitions)`. It needs `source`, which the failed read did not produce, so it depends on the failed check's result. Skipping it is therefore allowed.\n- The `continue` goes to the next file, so later files are still checked and their failures reported. This `continue` is harmless.\n\n**Parse failures.** The `Err` arm of `missing_function_references` records \"cannot parse Rust source\" and does not return. The loop carries on with later files, and the final progress update still runs.\n\n**No double reporting.**\n- Each failure is pushed once into `errors`.\n- The extra push into `discovery_errors` is a separate list for batch use. `inspect_repository_with_progress` (src/evidence.rs) builds `report.errors` only from `errors`, so the report does not repeat the message.\n- Reference discovery reports \"cannot read reference source\" for the same file in some read-failure cases. That message comes from another check in `collect_references_with_progress`, not from this function, and has different wording. It is outside this site.\n\nI found no gap.",
